<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archdiocese of Olomouc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1063">1063</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Bishopric of Olomouc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> is a Catholic see in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, established as a bishopric in 1063 and raised to an archbishopric in 1777. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were among the greatest landowners of Moravia and led German colonisation in the 13th century, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-of-schauenburg">Bruno of Schauenburg</a>. Their residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kromeriz">Kroměříž</a>, with its gardens, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archdiocese-of-olomouc.jpg" alt="Archdiocese of Olomouc" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michal Maňas · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dom_svateho_Vaclava.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
